     <p class="eyebrow">Home / Services / Life-cycle assessment</p>
     <h1>Building Life-Cycle Assessment in Egypt: Measure the Impact of Your Materials</h1>
     <p>Life-cycle assessment (LCA) measures the environmental impacts of a building’s materials and structure over a defined lifetime. WAHDJ builds a reference building and a proposed building in the Athena Impact Estimator, compares them over a 60-year life, and reports impacts using the TRACI 2.1 method.</p>
     <div class="facts"><strong>In short</strong>
      <p>WAHDJ compares reference and proposed whole-building material impacts over a 60-year life using the Athena Impact Estimator and TRACI 2.1.</p>
     </div>
    </div>
    <div class="article-copy">
     <h2>What LCA is used for</h2>
     <p>LEED v4.1 includes a whole-building life-cycle impact credit that rewards a proposed building with lower impacts than its reference. Comparing structural and envelope options shows which materials drive the impacts.</p>
     <h2>Impacts reported</h2>
     <p>TRACI 2.1 covers impact categories such as global warming, acidification, eutrophication, ozone depletion, smog formation and fossil fuel depletion. We report the results for the reference and proposed models side by side.</p>
     <h2>What you receive</h2>
     <p>Reference and proposed models, a comparison report over 60 years, bill of materials backup and credit documentation.</p>
     <h2>Where LCA fits in the single-contract model</h2>
     <p>This page shows our method; we do not publish a named LCA result. LCA sits beside <a href="#/en/services/energy-modeling">energy modeling</a> and <a href="#/en/services/daylight-analysis">daylight analysis</a> so the same team keeps the design data consistent.</p>
     <div class="facts"><strong>KEY FACTS</strong>
      <p>Tool: Athena Impact Estimator<br>
        Method: TRACI 2.1<br>
        Models: reference and proposed, 60-year life<br>
